---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="How a photo gets on screen — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="Opening one photo costs one, two or three decodes, and what decides it isn't the file format. The whole trip from a file on disk to pixels on your monitor."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-07-08">July 8, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>How a photo gets on screen</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          Opening one photo costs one, two or three decodes, and what decides
          it isn't the file format. The whole trip from a file on disk to
          pixels on your monitor.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 6 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          When you open a photo, the app runs the cheapest decode it can and
          then asks one question. Did that come back as big as the window
          needs? If yes, it stops there. If no, it queues a proper decode
          behind it. A third pass only happens if you zoom in past what's
          already on screen.
        </p>
        <p>
          After the decode, nothing touches the image data again. Zooming,
          panning and every slider are arithmetic on the graphics card.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Ask what you can show before the real work finishes.</strong>
          Cheap first, correct second, is what turns a 250 ms wait into a 35 ms
          one without making anything faster.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Let the result decide the next step, not the file
          type.</strong> I first branched on "is this a RAW", which sounds
          sensible and is the start of a long list of format special cases.
          Branching on "is what I got back big enough" is one rule that covers
          everything.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Upload once, then transform cheaply.</strong> If something
          repeats 60 times a second, it should change a few numbers, never
          re-run the expensive step.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Treat different waits as different problems.</strong>
          Opening, browsing and exporting look like one job called "decode a
          photo". One shared path would have served none of them well.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Fork low, converge high.</strong> Platform differences belong
          as close to the operating system as you can push them.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Three different jobs</h2>
      <p>
        I want LightPhotos to feel instant. You open a folder of holiday
        photos, the thumbnails fill in, you double-click one and it opens full
        size, you scroll the wheel and it zooms. None of that should surprise
        anyone, and that's exactly the point.
      </p>
      <p>
        A photo takes one of three paths through the app, and I treat them as
        three separate problems.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Opening.</strong> You clicked, and you want the photo large
          on screen right now.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Browsing.</strong> The grid needs a small version of every
          photo you can currently see.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Exporting.</strong> You want a JPEG on disk with your edits
          applied.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Opening is about the gap between your click and something appearing.
        Browsing is about doing one small job a hundred times without falling
        over. Exporting is about getting the pixels exactly right, and it can
        take as long as it likes, because you already pressed the button and
        walked away.
      </p>

      <h2>Opening a photo</h2>
      <p>
        A camera RAW file isn't an image. It's the raw readout from the sensor,
        and turning it into something you can look at takes real work. On my
        machine, demosaicing a Sony ARW down to screen size costs about 250 ms.
      </p>
      <p>
        Almost every RAW file comes with a shortcut, though. Your camera
        already wrote a small JPEG preview so it could show you the shot on its
        own screen. On that Sony the preview is 1616 pixels on the long side,
        and pulling it out takes about 35 ms.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the first pass is always the same thing. Get me the cheapest decode
        you can near the size I asked for, using the embedded preview if
        there's one worth having. Then comes the only question that matters.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="lp-pullquote">
        Did that come back as big as the window actually needs?
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        If it did, the app stops. One decode, and you're already looking at the
        final answer. That's every JPEG, because a JPEG has no embedded preview
        and the first pass simply decodes it at the size requested. It's also a
        RAW on a smaller window. If the loupe only needs 1400 pixels, that 1616
        pixel camera preview is already bigger than what fits, and the
        expensive decode never runs at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        If it came back short, the app queues a second decode at the real
        target and swaps it in when it lands. That's the case I hit most days,
        because my window asks for around 2560 pixels and the camera's preview
        is 1616.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 220" role="img" aria-label="Three cases. A JPEG, or a RAW whose preview is as big as the window, costs one decode and is done. A RAW whose preview is smaller than the window costs two, a 35 millisecond first pass on screen and a 250 millisecond second pass that swaps in. Zooming past the screen-sized version adds a third, full-resolution decode on demand. The rule is the size that came back, not the file format.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">HOW MANY DECODES ONE PHOTO COSTS</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="34">JPEG, or a RAW whose preview is as big as the window</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="0" y="42" width="180" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="90" y="59" text-anchor="middle">Pass 1</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="192" y="59">one decode, done</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="96">RAW whose preview is smaller than the window</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="104" width="180" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="90" y="121" text-anchor="middle">Pass 1 &middot; 35 ms</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="188" y="104" width="240" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="308" y="121" text-anchor="middle">Pass 2 &middot; 250 ms</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="440" y="121">two decodes</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="158">You zoom past the screen-sized version</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="166" width="180" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="90" y="183" text-anchor="middle">Pass 1</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="188" y="166" width="240" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="308" y="183" text-anchor="middle">Pass 2</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="436" y="166" width="172" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="522" y="183" text-anchor="middle">Pass 3 &middot; on demand</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s lp-fig-bad" x="0" y="212">The rule is the size that came back, not the file format.</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          Nothing here got faster. The app just found something worth showing
          while the expensive work was still running, and skipped that work
          entirely when it turned out not to be needed.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        I like this rule a lot more than the one I started with. Branching on
        the size that came back is a single rule that covers every format,
        including the ones I haven't added yet.
      </p>
      <p>
        The third pass is for zooming. Full-resolution decodes cost seconds and
        hundreds of megabytes, so that one only runs when you actually zoom in
        past the point where the screen-sized version still holds detail.
      </p>
      <p>
        There's one more detail I'm quietly pleased with. When you're arrowing
        through a shoot, the app pre-fetches the photos either side of you, but
        those only ever get the cheap first pass. Escalating a neighbour you
        haven't looked at yet would put it in a queue competing with the photo
        you're actually staring at. The moment you navigate onto it, it
        escalates like anything else.
      </p>

      <h2>After that, it lives on the GPU</h2>
      <p>
        Once a photo is decoded, it gets uploaded to the graphics card once.
        Zooming and panning never touch the image data again. They change a
        handful of numbers that get sent to the card each frame.
      </p>
      <p>
        The editing sliders work the same way. Drag Exposure and the new value
        goes to the GPU, where the shader recomputes the picture on the fly. No
        re-decode, no pixel loop on the CPU. You're looking at the original
        decode with some arithmetic applied on the way to the screen.
      </p>

      <h2>Two renderers</h2>
      <p>
        The buttons, grid, filmstrip and sliders are drawn by egui, an
        immediate-mode UI library. The photo itself is drawn by a few hundred
        lines of hand-written GPU code, sitting underneath a transparent panel.
      </p>
      <p>
        Splitting it this way lets me write the interface in the loose,
        convenient style egui encourages, while the part that has to be fast
        about pixels stays small enough to hand-tune. A general-purpose toolkit
        would have made the photo a widget, and widgets don't accept a
        24-megapixel texture with a custom shader without a fight.
      </p>

      <h2>Where the four builds meet</h2>
      <p>
        macOS, Linux, Windows and the browser build differ in exactly one
        place, which is how they turn a file into pixels. After that they all
        converge.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 280" role="img" aria-label="Diagram: four platform builds fork only at the decode layer, then converge into one shared edit pipeline feeding both the GPU tone shader and the exporter.">
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="0" width="140" height="30" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="70" y="20" text-anchor="middle">macOS</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="160" y="0" width="140" height="30" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="230" y="20" text-anchor="middle">Linux</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="320" y="0" width="140" height="30" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="390" y="20" text-anchor="middle">Windows</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="480" y="0" width="140" height="30" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="550" y="20" text-anchor="middle">Browser</text>

          <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M70 30 V52 M230 30 V52 M390 30 V52 M550 30 V52"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="52" width="140" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="70" y="73" text-anchor="middle">ImageIO</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="160" y="52" width="300" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="310" y="73" text-anchor="middle">Rust decoders</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="480" y="52" width="140" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="550" y="73" text-anchor="middle">same, in Web Workers</text>

          <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M70 86 V100 H550 M550 86 V100 M310 86 V152"></path>
          <line class="lp-fig-line-soft" x1="0" y1="116" x2="640" y2="116" stroke-dasharray="3 4"></line>
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="136">THE ONLY FORK. EVERYTHING BELOW IS SHARED</text>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M304 152 L316 152 L310 162 Z"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="90" y="166" width="460" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="320" y="187" text-anchor="middle">One edit function, in linear light</text>

          <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M200 200 V216 H440 M440 200 V216 M200 216 V230 M440 216 V230"></path>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M194 230 L206 230 L200 240 Z M434 230 L446 230 L440 240 Z"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="90" y="244" width="220" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="200" y="265" text-anchor="middle">Tone shader on the GPU</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="330" y="244" width="220" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="440" y="265" text-anchor="middle">Exported JPEG</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          Fork low, converge high. Every layer above the fork is a layer that
          can't drift between platforms.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The GPU tone shader is a direct translation of that one edit function.
        The function that bakes edits into finished pixels has exactly two
        callers, the exporter and the thumbnail you're looking at.
      </p>
      <p>
        That second fact is the whole reason an exported JPEG matches what you
        saw on screen. I never built that as a feature. It falls out of there
        being one code path, which is the kind of guarantee I'd much rather get
        for free than have to keep true by hand.
      </p>

      <p>
        If you want to see how this feels, there are builds for all four
        platforms on the <a href="/downloads.html">downloads page</a>.
      </p>

